### H2: Leading AI the same way you lead people

*Section purpose:* The leadership development angle — counter-intuitive and differentiated.*

#### H3: AI exposes your delegation gaps
- Using AI is delegation. Most people are bad at delegating. (Host's observation, validated by Clauddio)
- Symptoms: incomplete instructions, unconscious assumptions, "do this because I told you so"
- These failures surface immediately with AI because AI has no body language, no implicit context, no ability to fill gaps from social cues
- This is diagnostic information, not a flaw in the model

#### H3: AI as a risk-free leadership practice environment
- You wouldn't put a junior leader in charge of a real team to practice — but you can do this with AI
- Practicing the muscles: clear vision → enable → let it run → observe where it goes → fill the gap → have it restate its own instructions in its own words
- Clauddio's coaching loop applied to AI: "I tell it to do something. I watch it. I see where it's trying to look for information. I ask why it went that path. Then I give direction. Then I say: rewrite your instructions based on what I've told you. Let me see how you do it."
- The "wax on, wax off" principle: the repetition builds the skill before it matters

#### H3: What the hidden Excel tabs reveal about AI as an observer
- The accounting team story: AI read data the humans literally could not see (hidden tabs)
- The same dynamic applies to conflicting instructions, dead code, buried assumptions in documents
- Good leaders use what AI surfaces; bad prompts suppress it
- Asking AI "do you know a better way?" is a leadership habit — openness to information you didn't ask for

---

### H2: When new team members onboard themselves

*Section purpose:* The most concrete proof point. Stakes are real; outcome is measurable.*

#### H3: The precondition — 8 months of recorded work
- Every team interaction recorded: daily scrums, stakeholder conversations, everything
- Transcripts fed to AI → AI synthesized decision logs, challenge summaries, onboarding guides
- Clauddio did not write the documentation — he prompted AI to synthesize it from the transcripts

#### H3: Two new team members, no handholding
- Previous team member (who had helped build the AI tools) rolled off
- New members joined without knowledge of the tools, process, or mindset
- They used the documentation to self-onboard; came to Clauddio having already done the work
- Sprint review that Friday: stakeholders said "wow" about contributions from brand-new team members

#### H3: What made this possible — and what most teams skip
- The documentation habit had to exist *before* the need arose — 8 months of consistent recording
- The value is proportional to the upstream discipline; teams that record sporadically get sporadic benefit
- The team lead's role shifts: not a trainer, but a gap-filler for people who have already prepared

---

### H2: What this approach doesn't solve — and where it can go wrong

*Section purpose:* The Skeptic's View. Credible, fair, not a strawman.*

#### H3: This requires a facilitator with accumulated discipline
- The story-writing recipe Clauddio uses took years of coaching practice and blogging to build — it encodes his judgment, not a generic method
- A team starting from zero cannot simply download a markdown file and get the same results
- The "clarity of intent" discipline is a skill that must be practiced, not a tool that can be installed

#### H3: AI-generated clarity isn't the same as validated clarity
- A well-structured prompt can produce a well-structured story that's still wrong about the human need
- AI synthesizes what humans say — if the facilitated conversation didn't surface the real need, the story won't either
- Human stories still require human validation; the AI is a synthesis tool, not a truth engine

#### H3: When problem articulation creates unnecessary friction
- The need-problem-solution framework is most valuable at discovery phase
- Mid-execution, teams with clear scope don't need to restart from need articulation
- Forcing story-first thinking onto a team that already has clarity adds overhead, not value

